# THE REVIEW'S OWN EXAMPLE, now refused. `"256Mi" + "256Mi"` is the
# string "256Mi256Mi" and always will be: `+` is polymorphic and
# concatenation is one of its meanings (see quantity-concat.aon, still
# pinned). What the review's finding I asked for is a spelling that
# does NOT quietly answer -- and the arithmetic family is numeric, so
# `add` on two quantity strings is a located invalid-arg rather than
# nonsense that generates cleanly.
#
# The safe pattern is unchanged: keep the number and the unit apart,
# and now the number can be COMPUTED rather than written twice.
# run: aontu probes/quantity-add-refused.aon
# expected: [aontu/invalid-arg] at $.mem, exit 1
# actual:   [aontu/invalid-arg] at $.mem, exit 1
mem: add("256Mi", "256Mi")
